I basically took my old Redmi 8 and turned it into a custom music device for fun. 
The phone was originally limited to Android 10 because of its hardware architecture, but since it supports dynamic partitions, I used OrangeFox recovery to remap everything properly. 
That let me push the device way past its official limits and get it running Android 15, which honestly felt wild for a phone that old.

During the ROM experimenting phase, I tried multiple builds like CORE, STOCK, FULL, and OMNI versions of NikGApps just to see what worked. 
But for this project, I went with the HorizonDroid no-gapps version to keep the system completely clean and lightweight. 
Later, for basic app stability and general usage, I added only the essential NikGApps components because full gapps felt too heavy for what I wanted the device to be.

Once the system was stripped down and de-Googled, I set up Poweramp as the main app and locked the entire phone to run only that. 
No home screen, no notification shade, no distractions. 
The device literally boots straight into Poweramp, making it work like a dedicated music gadget. 
So the Redmi 8 basically went from an outdated phone to a DIY iPod-style music player with a modern Android base. 
Kind of satisfying watching a phone that was supposed to be dead become something new and actually useful.
